    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2028 Mitsubishi Outlander Diamond Trail Edition: How Does It Compare to Other Adventure Crossovers?\nBroken Tooling Prompts Ford F-Series Recall, 2026 and 2027 Models Affected\n8 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nAssembly line workers at Ford's Kentucky truck plant were running open-gate load tests on June 13, 2026. As part of said testing, plant operators noticed something worrying in the form of left-hand tailgate cables separating from the side eyelet. In the first instance, the Ford Motor Company hypothesized that the eyelet crimp was malformed.\nThe supplier, namely Ohio-based Cable Manufacturing & Assembly Co., ultimately confirmed that certain tailgate cables were produced using a broken crimping die. Potentially compromised parts were produced at the supplier's facility during two shifts on May 26, 2026, and the supplier also confirmed that only left-side cables may be compromised.\nBased on audit records, approximately 600 units of these suspect cables were shipped to the second-largest automaker among the Detroit Big Three. As expected, the Ford Motor Company issued a stop-ship order for the affected 2026 and 2027 model year F-Series trucks. The recall population includes the F-150 and Super Duties, all the way to the F-450.\nFord wants to inspect no fewer than 30,123 trucks in the US market, with production dates ranging from May 30, 2026, through June 15, 2026. The F-150 is listed with the largest population of the bunch, totaling 18,412 units across the 2026 and 2027 model years. The remainder splits among heavy-duty variants, of which the F-250 accounts for 7,387 units.\nFoMoCo estimates that only 2 percent of the entire population actually carries defective left-side cables. Imagine rolling a pair of motorcycles onto an open tailgate. The static weight hits both support cables. If the left-side tailgate eyelet pulls free, everything transfers to the right cable. The tailgate ultimately drops onto the rear bumper, no longer supporting the load, increasing the risk of injury.\nPhoto: Ford The Critical Concern Review Group analyzed this matter on June 18, 2026, and the Field Review Committee gave its blessing to recall potentially affected pickups three months later. To date, FoMoCo hasn't received a single warranty claim or field report potentially related to the recall condition.\nDealers are to swap out the suspect left-hand side tailgate cables at no charge to affected customers. The remedy units are said to be manufactured using properly calibrated tooling. Dealers will be notified about this recall, which the NHTSA refers to as 26V689, on January 11, 2027. Interim customer notifications are due this December, while remedy notifications are planned for January.\nAffected vehicle identification numbers will become searchable on the federal watchdog's portal no later than December 7, 2026. FL34-9944411-AD is the part number of the subject left-hand tailgate support cable. Based on a quick Google search, this part also fits the 2021 through 2025 model year F-150.\nFor model year 2027, the F-150 lost its 2.7-liter EcoBoost twin-turbo V6 in favor of a 3.0-liter unit. Elsewhere, the 5.0-liter Coyote naturally aspirated V8 returns to high-end trim levels. Automatic start-stop has been removed from a bunch of models, while a new gasoline particulate filter has been introduced to the 3.5-liter EcoBoost twin-turbo V6 and its PowerBoost-branded hybrid sibling.\nAs far as the Super Duty is concerned, the 2027 model year saw Ford eliminate the 6.8-liter gas V8 in favor of the larger Godzilla lump. As ever, the Power Stroke turbo diesel remains the Super Duty engine to get for towing. When properly equipped, the Super Duty offers up to 40,000 pounds (18,144 kilograms) as long as customers opt for the XL Regular Cab 4x2 DRW.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFord Ford F-150 Ford Super Duty recall safety\nDownload: Ford F-Series Tailgate Cable Recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 11 New Hot Wheels to Make You Happy Next Year",
